From 7585c2a919054f771600f94b1015cf4574e7e31c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Benjamin=20Cab=C3=A9?= <benjamin@zephyrproject.org>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:05:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] usb: host: usbip: survive device removal, name devices by
 their port
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The USB/IP service kept a pointer to the device it exported and was not
told when the device went away: a device that resets or is unplugged
during a session left the session thread and the transfer callbacks on
freed memory. Its bus ID was "1-<address>", and the address changes when
a device enumerates again: behind a hub, two devices that reset at the
same time can swap their bus IDs.

Each export context is now a host class instance. The stack binds it to
a device when it configures the device, and calls its removal handler
when the device goes: the handler cancels the transfers in flight and
ends the session, whose client sees the connection close. The device
list offers the devices so bound, which leaves out hubs, taken by the
hub class, and devices that failed to configure. The submit path holds
the context lock until the transfer is queued and cleans up when it
fails, a session that ends cancels what it left in flight, and the
completions of an earlier session are dropped.

A device is named by its place on the bus, as Linux does: "1-1" on the
root port, "1-1.3" on port 3 of a hub there, and so on. A device on the
root port keeps the bus ID it usually had.

(hw-testing-rack: the two upstream-ready commits squashed into one patch,
so that `west patch apply` can tell it is already applied.)

Assisted-by: Claude:opus-5.5
Signed-off-by: Benjamin Cabé <benjamin@zephyrproject.org>
---
